groups.description:
Proxies, Load Balancers, and CDNs within Network Protocols and the Request Lifecycle.

---

Advanced · **Must-know**

**Definition:** The layer between the public internet and your application that routes, terminates, secures, caches, and shapes traffic — from a forward proxy in front of a client, to a reverse proxy in front of your servers, to a globally distributed CDN.

**Forward vs reverse proxy**

| Aspect | Forward proxy | Reverse proxy |
|---|---|---|
| Sits in front of | clients | servers |
| Knows the client | typically yes (that's the point) | yes via `X-Forwarded-For`, but conceptually the server side |
| Purpose | egress control, anonymity, caching for a group of users, corporate policy | TLS termination, load balancing, caching, WAF, routing, compression |
| Direction | client → proxy → internet | internet → proxy → server |
| Who configures it | the user/admin of the client | the owner of the server side |
| Examples | corporate egress proxy, `CONNECT` tunneling, a library like `http_proxy` | Nginx, HAProxy, Envoy, Caddy, cloud ALBs, CDNs |

**What a reverse proxy does for a web app**
1. **TLS termination** — holds the certificate, does the handshake once for many clients, negotiates ALPN.
2. **Load balancing** — picks a healthy backend.
3. **Caching** — serves `Cache-Control`-compliant responses, and can revalidate upstream.
4. **Compression** — gzip/br/zstd on the fly.
5. **Static file serving** — much faster than your app for assets.
6. **Rate limiting and connection limiting** — `limit_req`, `limit_conn`, per-IP and per-key.
7. **Request routing / path rewriting** — map `/api` to a service, `/legacy` to a monolith.
8. **Header manipulation** — set `Host`, add `X-Forwarded-*`, inject auth from a trusted source.
9. **WAF / input filtering** — block obvious attacks, sanitise.
10. **Access logging and metrics** — a single structured log stream with timings.
11. **Timeouts and buffering** — controlling what's buffered and for how long.
12. **Blue/green and canary routing** — split traffic by weight or header.
13. **Health checks and draining** — take bad backends out and shut down good ones cleanly.

**L4 vs L7 balancing**

| Aspect | L4 (transport) | L7 (application) |
|---|---|---|
| Decision made on | IP, port, connection metadata | HTTP method, path, host, headers, cookies, TLS SNI |
| Sees the body | no | yes |
| Terminates TLS | no (passthrough) | yes |
| Latency overhead | very low | higher (parse, buffer, re-serialize) |
| Can cache / compress / rewrite | no | yes |
| Can route by path | no | yes |
| Protocol flexibility | any TCP/UDP (including QUIC with QUIC-aware LBs) | HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, gRPC, WebSocket |
| Loss of client IP | it sees the L4 source unless PROXY protocol is used | sees the real client IP directly |
| Certificate per hostname | no (backend holds it) | yes (proxy holds many) |
| Examples | AWS NLB, GCP L4, IPVS, HAProxy in tcp mode | AWS ALB, GCP HTTPS LB, Nginx, Envoy, HAProxy http mode |

**Load balancing algorithms**

| Algorithm | How it picks | Good for | Fails when |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Round robin** | next backend in order | backends with similar capacity and similar request cost | one backend is slow — it still gets equal share, and clients queue behind it |
| **Weighted round robin** | proportional to a weight | heterogeneous capacity | weights are static and guess at real load |
| **Least connections** | the backend with the fewest active connections | long-lived connections (WebSockets, SSE, gRPC streams) | requests have very different durations; a slow request still counts as one connection |
| **Least response time (least latency)** | lowest recent latency (EWMA) | backends that vary in speed or have noisy neighbours | latency estimates get noisy; can oscillate and cause herding |
| **IP hash** | hash(client IP) mod N | session affinity without cookies | breaks on backend count change (mass remapping), concentrates load behind CGNAT, ignores load |
| **Source port hash** | hash(src IP + src port) → fine-grained per-client | spreading a single CGNAT population | same remapping problem |
| **Consistent hashing** | hash on a ring, only affected keys move | large caches, sticky-ness that survives backend changes | needs many virtual nodes; doesn't consider load |
| **Weighted random** | random with weights | simple, fast, decent spreading | can be unlucky in bursts |
| **Least outstanding requests** | the backend with the fewest in-flight requests | request costs vary; better than least-connections for HTTP | needs accurate in-flight tracking including retries |
| **Power of two choices** | pick two at random, choose the shorter queue | excellent spreading at very low cost, used by many production balancers | needs per-backend queue length |

Best practice: a good default is **least-outstanding-requests** or **least-connections** for general traffic, plus **consistent hashing** when you have a large local cache, plus **hashing on a cookie or user ID** when you truly need affinity. And always pair with active health checks.

**Health checks**

| Aspect | Active | Passive |
|---|---|---|
| How | the balancer sends requests to a target on a schedule | the balancer watches real traffic for failures |
| Detects a "hung but listening" server | yes, if the check tests the right thing | no, until real users hit it |
| Cost | load on the target (choose interval and timeout carefully) | none |
| Time to notice a failure | interval + timeout | after enough real errors |
| False positives | possible if the check is too aggressive or hits a cache-warming path | possible if a single unlucky request evicts a good server |
| Cold start | needs a grace period so a booting server isn't killed instantly | — |

- **HTTP vs TCP checks:** a TCP check only proves the port is open. An HTTP check can assert on the status code and even on a body substring (`"ok":true`), which distinguishes "the process is up" from "the database is reachable." But a check that requires a database makes the server fail health checks during a database blip, which removes all capacity at once — a real anti-pattern. **Check liveness locally; check readiness for traffic.**
- **Slow start / ramp-up:** after a target passes its first check, its traffic weight should ramp up over tens of seconds so a cold cache and a JIT don't get hammered.
- **Hysteresis:** require N consecutive successes to mark healthy and M consecutive failures to mark unhealthy. With a 5 s interval, one failure threshold means a single dropped probe evicts a server and drops its in-flight work.
- Health check intervals trade detection speed against cost. A 1 s interval on 100 targets is 10,000 checks/s; that's a load generator you built.

**Sticky sessions — when they're actually required**
- Required when: the app keeps **session state in process memory** (no shared store), or in-memory websockets/caches must stay on one instance.
- Avoid when: you can make state external (Redis, DB) — then any load-balancing algorithm works and you scale horizontally.
- The theory of IP-hash affinity is broken by CGNAT (millions of users behind one IP), NAT rebinding, mobile networks that change IPs, and backend scaling events.
- Better approaches: a signed cookie or a token in the JWT identifying a logical session; consistent hashing on that value; or simply stateless tokens.
- **Sticky sessions hurt draining**: a sticky connection keeps a server alive, complicating deploys and rolling upgrades.

**Connection draining and graceful shutdown**
```
1. LB health check flips target to "draining" (or removes it from rotation)
2. LB stops sending NEW connections/requests to it
3. LB sends Connection: close (HTTP/1.1) or GOAWAY (HTTP/2) on in-flight responses
4. App receives SIGTERM, stops accepting new work, finishes in-flight work
5. App exits; LB removes the target once connections hit zero or a deadline passes
```
- In Kubernetes, this is `preStop` sleep + `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, with the readiness probe failing *before* the container is killed. The classic mistake is a readiness probe that only fails on SIGTERM, which arrives after the endpoint has already been removed from some load balancers.
- Long-lived connections (WebSockets, SSE, gRPC streams) need explicit draining: a "drain" signal that tells the app to send a `GOAWAY`/`Connection: close` and to let clients reconnect to a new instance.
- The failure without draining: a 502 storm, because the LB sends requests to a process that is exiting.

**`X-Forwarded-For` trust and spoofing**
```
client sends:  X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4
edge (honest) appends:  X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4, 5.6.7.8
app reading leftmost → believes 1.2.3.4 (attacker) — WRONG
app reading rightmost → 5.6.7.8 (edge) — rightmost non-trusted is the real client
```
- **The rightmost entry that is not from a trusted proxy is the closest thing to a real client IP** — but only if every hop in your infrastructure appends and no hop can be bypassed.
- Safer patterns: edge **overwrites** `X-Real-IP` and the app only accepts requests from known proxy IPs; or the edge uses the **PROXY protocol** (a layer 4 header) instead of an HTTP header, which is unauthenticated but can't be set by an external client because the LB strips/replaces it.
- Rate limiting by client IP is the biggest victim: spoofed `XFF` lets an attacker evade limits or frame someone else. If you're behind a CDN, the CDN's connecting IP is what you see; you need the CDN's real-client header (configured) and a trust list.
- With IPv6, keys must be normalised or the same client can be counted as many "different" addresses by privacy extensions.

**CDNs**
- **Pull vs push:** most CDNs are *pull* — the origin is pulled from on a cache miss (a "fill"). *Push* is pre-warming the edge by uploading content directly, used for very large or time-critical events (a livestream start, a product launch).
- **Cache key:** typically scheme + host + path + query string (+ sometimes selected headers, cookies, or `Vary`). The query string being part of the key is why `?utm_*` params destroy your hit rate, and why you either strip known tracking params at the edge or normalize the URL.
- **Vary handling:** if the origin sends `Vary: Accept-Encoding`, the CDN keys on it. If it sends `Vary: *` or varies on `Cookie`, you effectively have no caching.
- **TTL and revalidation:** the CDN honours `s-maxage` (shared cache) over `max-age` (browser). On expiry it either revalidates with the origin (`If-None-Match` → 304, cheap) or re-fetches. `stale-while-revalidate` serves stale content immediately and refreshes in the background, trading a little staleness for latency.
- **Sharding / shielding:** to avoid a cache miss at every edge (a "thundering herd" on the origin), a tiered CDN has a "shield" or "parent" tier; regional edges pull from the shield, not from the origin. Big win for unpopular content.
- **Geo and edge compute:** route by user geography, run small amounts of code (or a WASM/edge runtime) at the edge, and serve the nearest PoP.
- **Image optimization:** format negotiation (AVIF/WebP), resizing, quality tuning, and responsive `srcset` — a large, real, and often overlooked win.
- **Purge APIs:** invalidate by URL, by prefix/wildcard, by tag/selector, or purge everything. Purge-everything is dangerous on a large site and usually rate-limited.
- **Signed URLs / signed cookies:** token in the query or cookie proving entitlement; the edge validates the signature and expiry. This is how you serve paid or private content through a shared cache without the origin seeing every request. Implement with an HMAC over the path + expiry, validated at the edge.
- **CDN as DDoS control vs not:**
  - **Good at:** volumetric L3/L4 floods (anycast absorbs them), L7 request floods (WAF rules, rate limiting, bot management), and connection exhaustion (edge terminates and multiplexes).
  - **Not a silver bullet:** attacks that target the *application logic* (credential stuffing on a specific endpoint, expensive computation on a non-cacheable path, business-logic abuse), attacks on the *origin IP* if it's discoverable and not firewalled, Layer 7 attacks that look like valid requests from a distributed botnet, and attacks on the *origin's data*.
  - **Practical requirements:** make the CDN the *only* path to your origin (origin behind a firewall that only allows the CDN's published ranges), otherwise an attacker just goes direct.

**Nginx deep — a real, valid configuration**

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/nginx.conf (excerpt): worker processes and the basics
user  nginx;
worker_processes  auto;              # one worker per CPU core
error_log /var/log/nginx/error.log warn;
pid        /var/run/nginx.pid;

events {
    worker_connections  4096;        # per worker, simultaneous connections
    multi_accept        on;
    use                 epoll;        # Linux: epoll; macOS: kqueue
}

http {
    include       /etc/nginx/mime.types;
    default_type  application/octet-stream;

    # --- logging: structured enough to be parseable, with the timings that matter
    log_format  main  '$remote_addr - $remote_user [$time_local] "$request" '
                     '$status $body_bytes_sent "$http_referer" '
                     '"$http_user_agent" rt=$request_time uct=$upstream_connect_time '
                     'uht=$upstream_header_time urt=$upstream_response_time '
                     'rid=$request_id cs=$upstream_cache_status';
    access_log  /var/log/nginx/access.log  main;

    # --- compression
    gzip              on;
    gzip_vary         on;             # adds "Vary: Accept-Encoding"
    gzip_comp_level   5;
    gzip_min_length   1024;
    gzip_proxied      any;
    gzip_types        text/plain text/css text/javascript application/javascript
                      application/json application/xml application/rss+xml
                      image/svg+xml font/woff font/woff2;
    # note: never gzip application/octet-stream, images, video, or archives

    # --- transport tuning
    sendfile        on;               # sendfile(2) instead of read()+write()
    tcp_nopush      on;               # with sendfile, avoid the small-packet penalty
    tcp_nodelay     on;               # don't delay small responses (chatty apps)
    keepalive_timeout   65s;          # client idle timeout on the edge
    keepalive_requests  1000;         # recycle connections so memory/state is reclaimed
    reset_timedout_connection on;
    server_tokens   off;              # don't advertise the nginx version

    client_max_body_size   20m;       # reject bodies over this (413)
    client_body_timeout    30s;       # time to send the whole body
    client_header_timeout  15s;       # time to send headers
    large_client_header_buffers 4 16k; # roomy enough for big cookies

    # --- request id for cross-layer tracing
    map $http_x_request_id $request_id {
        default   $http_x_request_id;
        ""        $request_id;       # generated if the client didn't send one
    }

    # --- rate limiting zones
    # 10 MB shared memory, ~160k request states, 10 req/s sustained per key
    limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=perip:10m   rate=10r/s;
    # per authenticated user (falls back to IP)
    limit_req_zone $http_x_user_id    zone=peruser:10m rate=50r/s;
    # connections per IP
    limit_conn_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=connperip:10m;

    # --- upstream: least connections
    upstream api_backend {
        least_conn;                  # pick the server with the fewest active connections
        zone api_backend 64k;        # shared memory so workers agree on the choice

        server 10.0.1.11:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s weight=3;
        server 10.0.1.12:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s weight=3;
        server 10.0.1.13:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s weight=2;
        # 10.0.1.13:8080 backup;    # only used when all primaries are down

        keepalive 64;                # upstream keepalive pool (requires proxy_http_version 1.1)
        keepalive_timeout 60s;
        keepalive_requests 1000;
    }

    # static assets served directly by nginx
    upstream static_cdn {
        server 10.0.2.5:80;
        keepalive 32;
    }

    # HTTP -> HTTPS redirect
    server {
        listen 80;
        listen [::]:80;
        server_name example.com www.example.com;
        location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ { root /var/www/certbot; }
        location / { return 308 https://$host$request_uri; }
    }

    # main HTTPS vhost
    server {
        listen 443 ssl;
        listen [::]:443 ssl;         # dual-stack: binding :: (v6only=0) covers both
        http2 on;                    # nginx 1.25.1+; older: listen 443 ssl http2;

        server_name example.com;

        # --- TLS
        ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem;
        ssl_protocols       TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
        ssl_ciphers         ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305;
        ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off; # with TLS 1.3, the client chooses
        ssl_session_cache   shared:SSL:10m;
        ssl_session_timeout 1d;
        ssl_session_tickets off;      # tickets off; resumption via the shared session cache
        ssl_stapling        on;
        ssl_stapling_verify on;
        ssl_trusted_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/chain.pem;

        add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains" always;
        add_header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff always;
        add_header Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin always;

        root /var/www/app;
        index index.html;

        # --- rate limits
        limit_req  zone=perip  burst=20 nodelay;
        limit_conn connperip 50;

        # --- SPA: serve index.html for client-side routes
        location / {
            try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
        }

        # --- API
        location /api/ {
            limit_req zone=peruser burst=100;

            proxy_pass         http://api_backend/;   # trailing slash strips /api/ before upstream
            proxy_http_version 1.1;                  # required for upstream keepalive
            proxy_set_header   Connection "";         # clear hop-by-hop header -> enables keepalive
            proxy_set_header   Host              $host;
            proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
            proxy_set_header   X-Request-Id      $request_id;

            proxy_connect_timeout 2s;
            proxy_send_timeout    30s;
            proxy_read_timeout    30s;
            proxy_buffering       on;
            proxy_buffer_size     8k;
            proxy_buffers         16 8k;
            proxy_busy_buffers_size 16k;

            proxy_next_upstream error timeout http_502 http_503 http_504;
            proxy_next_upstream_tries 2;
        }

        # --- streaming (SSE): disable buffering, raise the read timeout
        location /api/stream {
            proxy_pass http://api_backend/;
            proxy_http_version 1.1;
            proxy_set_header Connection "";
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_buffering off;                 # otherwise events sit in the buffer
            proxy_cache off;
            proxy_read_timeout 3600s;
            chunked_transfer_encoding on;
        }

        # --- WebSocket upgrade
        location /ws {
            proxy_pass http://api_backend/;
            proxy_http_version 1.1;
            proxy_set_header Upgrade    $http_upgrade;
            proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
            proxy_set_header Host       $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_read_timeout 3600s;            # don't kill idle WebSockets
        }

        # --- static assets
        location ~* \.(?:css|js|jpg|jpeg|png|gif|webp|avif|svg|woff2?|ttf|eot)$ {
            expires 1y;                         # immutable if you content-hash filenames
            add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
            add_header Vary Accept-Encoding;
            access_log off;
            try_files $uri =404;
        }

        # --- metrics only from inside
        location /nginx_status {
            stub_status;                        # Active/handles/requests/reading/writing/waiting
            access_log off;
            allow 10.0.0.0/8;
            deny  all;
        }
    }
}
```

**`stub_status` output and what it means**
```
Active connections: 291
server accepts handled requests
 16630948 16630948 31070465
Reading: 6 Writing: 179 Waiting: 106
```
- `Active connections` = accepted − handled + waiting.
- `Reading` = nginx is reading the client's request (slow clients).
- `Writing` = nginx is sending a response back (client is slow to read, or the response is large).
- `Waiting` = keepalive connections with no active request. A very large `Waiting` number relative to your app's capacity is fine; a very large `Writing` number means clients aren't reading.

**Nginx config test and reload**
```bash
nginx -t                                  # syntax + referenced files check
nginx -T                                  # dump the full effective config (great for debugging)
nginx -s reload                           # graceful: old workers finish in-flight, new workers take new connections
nginx -s quit                             # graceful shutdown
systemctl reload nginx
```

**Caddy's automatic HTTPS**
```
example.com {
    encode gzip zstd
    reverse_proxy /api/* localhost:8080 {
        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
        header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
    }
    handle /ws {
        reverse_proxy localhost:8080 { flush_interval -1 }
    }
    file_server
    tls {
        issuer acme {
            email ops@example.com
        }
    }
}
```
- Caddy obtains and **renews** certificates automatically via ACME (Let's Encrypt, ZeroSSL, or an internal CA), handles the HTTP-01 or TLS-ALPN-01 challenge, redirects HTTP to HTTPS, and reloads itself on renewal.
- Its `reverse_proxy` supports HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, and WebSockets transparently, and it's a genuinely pleasant default for a small service.
- **Trade-offs vs Nginx:** less ecosystem muscle for exotic tuning, fewer of the deep per-request knobs, and a config model that's opinionated. Nginx wins for very high-traffic, deeply tuned, or heavily customised set-ups; Caddy wins for "it just works with TLS and I don't want to think about it."

**Nginx vs HAProxy vs Envoy vs cloud LBs**

| Aspect | Nginx | HAProxy | Envoy | Cloud LBs (ALB/NLB/etc.) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Primary role | web server + reverse proxy + L7 LB | L4/L7 LB, TLS termination, "the load balancer" | L7 proxy, service-mesh data plane | managed L4/L7 LB |
| Strengths | static files, simple configs, huge ecosystem, great all-rounder | fastest mature L4, superb health checks, ACLs, great for TCP | xDS dynamic config, outlier detection, mesh-native, strong observability | zero ops, auto-scaling, integrated with the platform's networking |
| Weaknesses | less sophisticated LB algorithms, config is imperative | no static file serving, no gzip of responses, awkward for serving content | complex to run directly, heavy for simple sites | vendor lock-in, less visibility, cost |
| Best fit | single origin with a few routes; static + dynamic | many TCP services, very high connection rates, precise traffic shaping | large fleets, multi-team, service mesh | most teams, until you need control |
| Config style | files, `nginx -t` then reload | files, runtime API for dynamic changes | static bootstrap + xDS from a control plane | console/CLI/API, IaC |
| Memory for keepalive state | moderate | low | higher per connection (more features) | n/a |

**Questions**
1. Forward vs reverse proxy: three differences that matter operationally.
2. Enumerate ten things a reverse proxy does that your application should not have to.
3. L4 vs L7: what can an L7 balancer do that an L4 one cannot, and what does it cost?
4. Compare round robin, least connections, least response time, IP hash, and consistent hashing. When is each correct?
5. Why is IP-hash affinity unreliable in the real world?
6. Active vs passive health checks, and what each misses.
7. HTTP vs TCP health checks, and why a database-dependent health check is dangerous.
8. What are slow start and hysteresis, and what breaks without them?
9. When is sticky session the right answer, and how do you avoid needing it?
10. Walk graceful shutdown behind a load balancer, including the HTTP/2 and WebSocket cases.
11. A client sends `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4`. What does your app see, and what's the correct way to determine the real IP?
12. What is the PROXY protocol and when is it better than `X-Forwarded-For`?
13. Pull vs push CDN, and when would you push?
14. What is the CDN cache key, and how do tracking query parameters destroy it?
15. Explain shielding and the thundering-herd problem it solves.
16. What does `stale-while-revalidate` trade away?
17. How do signed URLs work, and what do they let you do that cookies alone don't?
18. When is a CDN a DDoS control, and when is it not?
19. What must be true about your origin's firewall for the CDN to actually protect it?
20. In the Nginx config above, what does `proxy_set_header Connection "";` do, and why is `proxy_http_version 1.1` needed with it?
21. What do `uht`, `urt`, and `uct` in the log format tell you, and how do you use them to attribute latency?
22. Why does `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;` work for an SPA, and what must be configured for deep links?
23. What is the danger of `proxy_buffering on` for SSE?
24. Read the `stub_status` output above. What does `Writing: 179` suggest?
25. When would you choose Caddy over Nginx, and when the reverse?
26. Nginx vs HAProxy vs Envoy — how do you choose, and what does each optimize for?

**Model answer, Q18 (senior framing: Context → Decision → Reasoning → Trade-offs → Failure scenario → Alternative):**
> "Context: someone asks whether putting a CDN in front makes us DDoS-proof. It doesn't, and believing it does is how companies get taken down.
>
> Decision: I'd use the CDN as one layer, and specifically for the layers it's actually good at, while making sure the origin is not directly reachable and adding application-layer defences that the CDN can't do for me.
>
> Reasoning: A CDN is extraordinarily good at absorbing *volumetric and connection-level* attacks because it is anycast across thousands of locations with enormous aggregate bandwidth — a 300 Gbps flood simply doesn't have a single pipe to fill, and even a per-IP flood is spread across every edge. It's also good at *generic L7 request floods* because it can rate limit, challenge, and run a WAF with rules I don't have to operate. What it cannot do is understand my business logic. A credential-stuffing attack with valid-looking, correctly-formatted login attempts at a rate below the WAF's threshold will pass straight through to my login endpoint and consume my database connections. Similarly, an expensive report endpoint, a search query that triggers a pathological plan, or a "create order" endpoint that lets one account do 10,000 orders a minute is an application-layer abuse problem, not a network problem — and the CDN will happily forward it.
>
> Trade-offs: the CDN adds a hop, a cache-invalidation surface, and a dependency on a third party. Very aggressive bot management and rate limiting can block legitimate users. And a very low origin TTL on hot content can turn a cache stampede into an origin outage.
>
> Failure scenario: the failure I'd most want to avoid is having the CDN in front but leaving the origin publicly reachable. The attacker finds the origin IP — from a historical DNS record, a leaked config, a direct subdomain, an `MX`, or a scan — and bypasses the entire edge. Every incident report I've read of this shape says the same thing: the CDN was there, the origin was open. The fix is a security group or firewall that accepts only the CDN's published ranges, plus a health-check path that still works from the CDN's ranges.
>
> Alternative: pair the CDN with rate limiting that is keyed on identity rather than IP (a session token or API key, not `X-Forwarded-For`), with a WAF rule set, with per-account quotas in the application, and with an origin that fails safely. If the business is attractive enough to be targeted, the real controls are in the app and the data layer, not the edge."

**Mistakes:** trusting the leftmost `X-Forwarded-For`; assuming a CDN means DDoS immunity; health checking a dependency and cascading an outage; using IP-hash affinity and calling it session stickiness; not setting `proxy_http_version 1.1` and wondering why upstream keepalive never engages; leaving `proxy_buffering on` for SSE and debugging phantom latency.

