topics.referenceMarkdown:
🟢 Beginner · **Must-know**

**Definition.**

- **Clickjacking**: a transparent overlay makes a user click something they cannot see. Modern browsers largely mitigate this with CSP `frame-ancestors`, so the modern defense is the header, not JavaScript.
- **CORS**: a browser-enforced policy telling a page whether it may *read* a cross-origin response. It is a client-side read control; it says nothing about whether the request was authorized.
- **Open redirect**: an attacker-controlled `next`/`returnUrl` that sends a victim from your trusted domain to a phishing site — defeating user training and bypassing email/link allow-lists.

**Clickjacking.**

```nginx
# Preferred: CSP frame-ancestors, one source of truth
Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
# or, when you must be embeddable by known partners:
Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self' https://partner.example.com https://*.trusted-cdn.com

# Legacy fallback for old browsers
X-Frame-Options: DENY            # or SAMEORIGIN
```

`frame-ancestors` **overrides** `X-Frame-Options` when both are present in modern
browsers. Use `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN` as a belt-and-suspenders fallback. Note the
common error: `frame-ancestors 'self'` does not allow being framed by a *subdomain*, and
`https://*.example.com` does not match the apex — list both if you mean both. Also note
that `frame-ancestors` does not work in `meta` tags with `report-uri` in some cases — set
it as a real header.

**CORS.** The mechanics, and the exact misconfigurations that matter.

```js
// Reflect an arbitrary origin with credentials = the classic catastrophic misconfiguration
app.use(cors({
  origin: req => req.headers.origin,               // NEVER
  credentials: true,
}));
```

```js
// Correct: explicit allow-list, evaluated exactly, with `Vary: Origin` always set
const ALLOWED_ORIGINS = new Set([
  'https://app.example.com',
  'https://admin.example.com',
]);

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const origin = req.get('origin');
  res.setHeader('Vary', 'Origin');
  if (origin && ALLOWED_ORIGINS.has(origin)) {      // exact string compare, not prefix/regex
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', origin);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', 'true');
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Authorization, Content-Type, X-CSRF-Token');
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS');
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Max-Age', '600');
  }
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') return res.status(204).end();
  next();
});
```

**The misconfiguration catalogue.**

| Misconfiguration | Risk |
|---|---|
| Reflect any origin + `credentials: true` | Any site can read authenticated responses as the user. Equivalent to a full account compromise for anything browser-readable. |
| Prefix match: `origin.startsWith('https://example.com')` | `https://example.com.evil.tld` passes |
| Regex without escapes: `/example\.com/` | Same, plus `re.test()` is not anchored |
| `*.example.com` in a library that also matches the apex and any port/protocol variant | Widen the surface |
| `null` origin allowed | Sandboxes, `file://`, some redirect-based flows |
| Wildcard `*` with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` | Browsers reject it — which means it is a misconfiguration *you* will debug and maybe "fix" incorrectly by reflecting the origin |
| Reflecting the `Origin` for a request authenticated by a **bearer token in JS** | Less severe, but you are handing any site the ability to use the token if they have it |
| Missing `Vary: Origin` | **Cache poisoning**: a CDN caches a response with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: attacker.tld` and serves it to real users |
| Preflight cached too long without `Vary` | Same, on `OPTIONS` |
| `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: *` with credentials | Broadens what any allowed origin can send |

**CORS is not a substitute for anything.** It does not protect a server-to-server
request (curl has no Origin), does not prevent CSRF *requests* from being sent (only from
being read), and does not stop a malicious site from *causing* an action.

**Open redirects.**

```js
// VULNERABLE
res.redirect(req.query.next);

// SAFE: allow-list of path prefixes, then build a same-origin relative URL
const ALLOWED_PREFIXES = ['/dashboard', '/settings', '/orders', '/billing'];
export function safeReturnTo(raw, { fallback = '/dashboard' } = {}) {
  if (typeof raw !== 'string' || raw.length > 200) return fallback;
  // Reject scheme-relative (//evil.tld) and backslash tricks (/\evil.tld)
  if (raw.startsWith('//') || raw.startsWith('/\\') || raw.includes('\\')) return fallback;
  // Only same-origin relative paths that are in the allow-list
  if (!ALLOWED_PREFIXES.some((p) => raw === p || raw.startsWith(p + '/'))) return fallback;
  // Also reject anything that could re-parse as absolute
  try {
    const u = new URL(raw, 'https://app.example.com');
    if (u.origin !== 'https://app.example.com') return fallback;
    return u.pathname + u.search;
  } catch { return fallback; }
}
res.redirect(safeReturnTo(req.query.next));
```

The same care is required for **host injection in absolute URLs** — password-reset
emails and OAuth redirect URIs must derive the host from configuration, never from
`Host`/`X-Forwarded-Host`, or an attacker can generate a reset link pointing at their
domain. Validate against a fixed allow-list of hosts, and only trust forwarding headers
from a known proxy.

**When to use vs when not to.**

- `frame-ancestors 'none'` unless you genuinely need embedding; if you do, list exact origins.
- CORS: disable it entirely for APIs that no browser calls. `same-origin` default is the secure default; add allowances one at a time.
- CORS allow-lists in configuration, not code, so they can be reviewed and changed without a deploy.
- Open redirects: never for authentication callbacks. Always for post-login navigation, and always allow-list.

**Trade-offs.**

- `X-Frame-Options` is understood by everything but only supports same-origin/none; `frame-ancestors` supports per-origin allow-lists. Ship both.
- A strict CORS allow-list breaks legitimate browser clients; the fix is a first-party origin or a BFF, not a wider allow-list.
- Allow-listing redirect paths degrades deep-linking UX; mitigate with a short-lived, signed "intent token" for complex flows.

**Production considerations.**

- **Test CORS from the browser**, not from curl. A shell test proves nothing about preflight, credentials, and `Vary`.
- **CDN rules**: strip or normalize `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` at the edge so a misconfigured origin cannot be cached.
- Log blocked/allowed CORS decisions with the Origin value; an origin you have never seen requesting credentials is worth an alert.
- `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin` and `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` for anything with credentials.
- Redirect allow-lists should be centralized and unit-tested with a hostile corpus:
  `//evil.tld`, `/\evil.tld`, `/%2f%2fevil.tld`, `https:/\/\evil.tld`, `http://evil.tld`,
  `/dashboard/../../etc`, CRLF payloads, and unicode homoglyphs.

**Common mistakes.**

1. Reflecting `Origin` with credentials.
2. Missing `Vary: Origin`.
3. `startsWith` origin matching.
4. `frame-ancestors 'self'` while depending on subdomain embedding (or the reverse).
5. Open redirect on the OAuth callback, which turns a legitimate IdP login into a phishing launchpad.
6. Building absolute URLs from `Host` in emails.
7. Testing CORS with `curl` only.

**Interviewer probes.**

- "Why is `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with credentials dangerous rather than merely useless?" (The browser refuses to attach credentials to a wildcard ACAO, so it looks "safe but broken" — and the tempting fix is to reflect the request's `Origin`, which is exactly the vulnerability. The correct fix is an explicit allow-list.)
- "How do you support an embeddable widget on a partner domain without giving up clickjacking protection?" (`frame-ancestors` listing only that partner; keep `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN` as fallback; keep the session cookie `SameSite=None` **only if** the widget genuinely needs it, with CSRF tokens and exact `Origin` checks; and do not let the widget frame a page that exposes sensitive actions without additional interaction confirmation.)

